Subject: [PATCH] arch/x86/lib/memmove_64.c: Convert it to assembly file and fix return value bug
Date: Mon, 17 Jan 2011 17:39:15 -0800
Message-Id: <1295314755-6625-1-git-send-email-fenghua.yu@intel.com>
X-Mailer: git-send-email 1.7.2
Sender: linux-kernel-owner@vger.kernel.org
List-ID: <linux-kernel.vger.kernel.org>
X-Mailing-List: linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

From: Fenghua Yu <fenghua.yu@intel.com>

memmove_64.c only implements memmove() function which is mainly written in
inline assembly code. Therefore it doesn't make sense to keep the assembly code
in .c file. The converted code don't have push rbp, mov rsp, rbp, leaveq
instructions generated by gcc at the beginning and end of memmove(). Thus the
function is a bit shorter and faster.

Currently memmove() doesn't store return value to rax. This may cause issue if
caller uses the return value. The patch fixes this issue.

Signed-off-by: Fenghua Yu <fenghua.yu@intel.com>
